WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.490
Reducing extraneous load means removing presentation friction that does not support

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:05.160
learning.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:10.090
Cluttered slides, decorative graphics, unnecessary detail, weak examples, unclear labels,

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:12.155
and poor pacing all consume attention.

00:00:12.155 --> 00:00:13.320
Learners may still work hard,

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.765
but their effort goes into sorting through the material rather than understanding the core

00:00:16.765 --> 00:00:17.600
relationship.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.604
Clean design is not about making a course plain.

00:00:19.604 --> 00:00:22.560
It is about making the important structure visible.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.860
Split attention is a common source of extraneous load.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:28.630
It happens when learners must divide attention between poorly integrated sources of

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:29.160
information,

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.625
such as a diagram on one part of the page and the explanation somewhere else.

00:00:32.625 --> 00:00:36.900
The learner has to search, match, and remember pieces before understanding can begin.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:40.165
Integrating labels, placing explanations close to the relevant visual,

00:00:40.165 --> 00:00:43.280
and aligning narration with the diagram can reduce that burden.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.120
Redundancy can also create load.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.210
Repeating the same information in unnecessary forms may seem helpful,

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:50.400
but it can force learners to process clutter.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:51.010
For example,

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:54.970
reading dense on-screen text word for word while the learner sees the same text can become

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:55.720
inefficient.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.960
The issue is not that repetition is always bad.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:02.210
The issue is whether each representation adds value or merely competes for attention.

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:04.070
Navigation and pacing matter as well.

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:06.965
If learners are uncertain where to click, what section they are in,

00:01:06.965 --> 00:01:09.080
or how examples relate to the current concept,

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:11.920
working memory is spent on the interface instead of the idea.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:15.010
Strong materials help learners focus on the essential relationship:

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:17.715
what is being explained, why it matters, how the parts connect,

00:01:17.715 --> 00:01:19.680
and what the learner should be able to do with it.
